  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-213304",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "Recall that a linear congruence $ax≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)$ has a unique solution modulo $n$ if and only if $\\text{gcd}(a,n)=1.$\n\nIn the given congruence, we have $a=6$ and $n=25$, where\n\n$$\n\\text{gcd}(a,n)=\\text{gcd}(6,25)=1.\n$$\n\nSo, our congruence does indeed have a unique solution.\n\nTo find the solution, we need to multiply both sides of the equation by the multiplicative inverse of $6\\,(\\text{mod}\\,25).$ To find the inverse of $6\\,(\\text{mod}\\,25)$, we compute the exponents of $6\\,(\\text{mod}\\,25)$ until we get a result of $1\\,(\\text{mod}\\,25):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{6}^{2} & ≡36≡11 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,25) \\\\ {6}^{3} & ≡11\\cdot 6≡66≡16 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,25) \\\\ {6}^{4} & ≡16\\cdot 6≡96≡21 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,25) \\\\ {6}^{5} & ≡21\\cdot 6≡126≡1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,25)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $21\\cdot 6≡1\\,(\\text{mod}\\,25),$ which means that $21$ is the multiplicative inverse of $6$ (modulo $25$).\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of our congruence by $21,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6x & ≡10 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,25) \\\\ 21\\cdot 6x & ≡21\\cdot 10 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,25) \\\\ (21\\cdot 6)x & ≡210 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,25) \\\\ 1\\cdot x & ≡10+8\\cdot 25 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,25) \\\\ x & ≡10 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,25)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x≡10\\,(\\text{mod}\\,25).$",
